---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'About Hours Apart — a practical world clock';
const description =
  'Why Hours Apart exists, what it helps with, and how it is maintained as a practical tool for people coordinating across time zones.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/about">
  <article class="max-w-2xl">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] text-paper-500 uppercase">About</p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-4xl">
        A world clock for the human part of time zones
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        Hours Apart is an independent tool created and maintained by Bytefish Studios for people who
        work, travel, study or keep in touch across cities. It answers the question behind a
        conversion: not only what time it is there, but whether it is a sensible hour to call.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What the tool is for</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        A normal time converter gives an offset for one moment. That is useful, but it misses the
        practical questions: Is it daylight? Is this inside a colleague&apos;s working day? Will a
        weekly meeting shift when one city changes its clocks? Hours Apart brings those questions
        into one view with a world clock, a meeting planner and city-pair explanations.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Built around real scheduling decisions</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        The site focuses on cities people commonly compare rather than trying to list every possible
        time-zone label. City pages show the current local time, daylight-saving transitions and an
        approximate daylight pattern. Pair pages show every gap that occurs across the year and the
        dates it changes. The meeting planner lets you model working hours before sending an invite.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">How it is maintained</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        Hours Apart is actively maintained as a small web product. The city list, explanations,
        daylight-saving wording and navigation are reviewed as the tool develops. Changes that
        affect the service are recorded in the <a
          href="/changelog"
          class="ring-focus rounded font-medium text-paper-900 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-paper-700"
          >changelog</a
        >. The underlying methods and their limits are explained in <a
          href="/methodology"
          class="ring-focus rounded font-medium text-paper-900 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-paper-700"
          >how Hours Apart works</a
        >.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Feedback makes the tool better</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        If a city is missing, a transition looks wrong or a planning flow is awkward, send a
        specific example through the <a
          href="/contact"
          class="ring-focus rounded font-medium text-paper-900 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-paper-700"
          >contact page</a
        >. A share link, the cities involved and the date being checked are usually enough to
        reproduce a problem.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
